  "subtitle": "The first domestic computer that sewed",
  "why_scout_cares": "A microprocessor-driven home sewing machine with an LCD readout, push-button stitch selection, and user-programmable stitch memory — the first domestic embedded computer the collection has ever had. Millions of households, mostly women, interacted daily with a programmable memory-equipped machine years before 'ubiquitous computing' had a name. Genuinely 'commercially odd': a computer that sews, whose programming model visibly bleeds into the physical world (stitch-count buttonholes that drift when the fabric bunches). Fills a completely empty niche.",
